<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oda Nobunaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oda Nobunaga was a Japanese warlord of the sixteenth century who began the military unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> served as his ally. After Nobunaga's death in 1582, his work was continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and completed by Ieyasu, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="Oda Nobunaga" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 狩野宗秀 (Kanō Sōshū, 1551 - 1601) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odanobunaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oda Nobunaga was born on 23 June 1534 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, the heir of Oda Nobuhide, head of the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda clan</a> and a deputy shugo, and his lawful wife. He is traditionally said to have been born at Nagoya Castle, though recent scholars favour Shobata Castle. As a youth he was known for bizarre dress and eccentric behaviour and was nicknamed Ōutsuke, 'great fool'. When Nobuhide died unexpectedly in 1551, part of the clan opposed Nobunaga's succession; he assembled a force of 1,000 men to intimidate his enemies, and in 1552 he repelled an attack by his uncle Oda Nobutomo, who had the support of the governor of Owari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1560 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> with an army of 25,000 men, and the clash weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, which lost control of the Matsudaira clan. In 1561 Nobunaga allied with Matsudaira Motoyasu, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. After defeating Saitō Tatsuoki, he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mino-province">Mino Province</a> in 1567 and then Ise. He entered Kyoto in honour of the Ashikaga shogun Yoshiaki, whom he installed, but a coalition formed against him, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-yoshikage">Asakura Yoshikage</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> and the militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a> movement, which was centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a> sect. Nobunaga overthrew Yoshiaki and ended the Ashikaga Shogunate in 1573.</p><p>He built Azuchi Castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> between 1576 and 1579; screens depicting it were given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a>. He fought the Mōri clan and the Uesugi clan, conquered Iga in 1581, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> destroyed the Takeda at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tenmokuzan">Battle of Tenmokuzan</a> in 1582. Historians note that he was among the first daimyō to use large numbers of firearms, and that he encouraged free trade. He used the seal Tenka Fubu, 'spreading military power throughout the world', to express his aim of ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1582 Nobunaga controlled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. That year his retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> ambushed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, and Nobunaga committed seppuku on 21 June. Toyotomi Hideyoshi defeated Mitsuhide at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> and completed the unification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. The period of Nobunaga's and Hideyoshi's power is called the Azuchi–Momoyama period, after Nobunaga's Azuchi Castle and Hideyoshi's Fushimi Castle at Momoyama. Nobunaga's rule is also remembered for the brutal suppression of those who would not yield to him. According to the Jesuit Luís Fróis, he held no particular religious belief but respected religious institutions that stayed out of politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oda Nobunaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1565 the shōgun Ashikaga Yoshiteru was assassinated, and the daimyō <b>Oda Nobunaga</b> used the opening to install Yoshiteru's brother Ashikaga Yoshiaki as shōgun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He survived as an ally of <b>Oda Nobunaga</b> and then a vassal of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, building up resources in his Kanto domains around Edo and avoiding premature ambition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At Nagashino in 1575 the massed arquebusiers of <b>Oda Nobunaga</b> and Tokugawa Ieyasu broke the charges of the Takeda cavalry, and the warlords who mastered firearms went on to unify the country.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>Shingen was seeking a route to Kyoto in his campaign against <b>Oda Nobunaga</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Asakura Yoshikage sheltered the future shogun Ashikaga Yoshiaki and allied with the Azai against <b>Oda Nobunaga</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/asakura-clan-domain">Asakura clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Sukemasa's son Hisamasa was overshadowed by the Rokkaku of southern Omi, but Nagamasa defeated them in 1560 and married Oichi, sister of <b>Oda Nobunaga</b>, around 1567, an alliance that secured Nobunaga's route to Kyoto.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shobata Castle. Died in former site of Honnō-ji, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.75&amp;year=1565" aria-label="former site of Honnō-ji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Oda Nobunaga"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4012 4511h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4012 4511h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.75&amp;year=1565">What was here in 1565? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
